mcgregor-wm for Slackware 3.6
=============================

This is mcgregor-wm, an extremely minimal Blackbox-style window manager,
built for a 1998 Slackware 3.6 system: Linux 2.0, libc5, gcc 2.7.2.3 and
XFree86 3.3 (X11R6.3). It is one C file that needs only Xlib.

What it does:

  - Each window gets a frame with a title bar and an [X] close box. There
    is no toolbar and there are no menus.
  - 4 desktops.
  - New windows never take the keyboard focus from the window you're
    typing in. They appear with an orange title bar, one Alt+Tab away.
  - Alt+Tab cycles windows in most-recently-used order, and the mouse
    pointer jumps to the window you pick.
  - Resize by dragging any edge or corner.
  - Restart in place without ending X.


Differences from the modern version
-----------------------------------

  - Titles are drawn with a core X font (9x15 if present, else 8x13 or
    7x13, else "fixed") in Latin-1. XFree86 3.3 can't draw UTF-8, so the
    rare app that sets a UTF-8 title has it converted; characters outside
    Latin-1 show as '?'.
  - The screen is one monitor. Xinerama didn't exist until XFree86 4.0.
    (On a later system that has it, build with
    make CFLAGS="-O2 -Wall -DXINERAMA" LDLIBS="-lX11 -lXinerama"
    to get the multi-monitor support back.)
  - Alt+Return starts xterm.
  - The autostart file is ~/.mcgregor-wm/autostart.
  - The code is plain C89, as gcc 2.7.2.3 expects.


Building and installing
-----------------------

Copy mcgregor-wm-slack36.tar.gz to the Slackware machine (ftp, floppy,
whatever works), then as root:

    gzip -dc mcgregor-wm-slack36.tar.gz | tar xf -
    cd mcgregor-wm-slack36
    make
    make install          # /usr/local/bin/mcgregor-wm, /usr/local/man/man1

You need the X development files: the "x" disk set's xbin and xlib
packages put the headers in /usr/X11R6/include and the libraries in
/usr/X11R6/lib, which the Makefile uses. If your X is elsewhere:

    make XINC=-I/path/to/include XLIB=-L/path/to/lib

To use it, put this at the end of ~/.xinitrc and run startx:

    exec mcgregor-wm

With xdm, put the same line at the end of ~/.xsession (and make that
file executable).


Keys
----

    Alt+Tab, Alt+Shift+Tab   cycle windows, most recent first; release Alt
                             to choose, Esc cancels
    Alt+1 .. Alt+4           go to desktop 1-4
    Alt+Shift+1 .. 4         send the window to desktop 1-4
    Ctrl+Alt+Left / Right    previous / next desktop
    Alt+F4                   close
    Alt+F9                   iconify (Alt+Tab brings it back)
    Alt+F10                  maximize / restore
    Alt+F11                  fullscreen / restore
    Alt+Return               xterm
    Ctrl+Alt+Shift+R         restart mcgregor-wm (X keeps running)
    Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Q         quit

Mouse:

    click a window           focus and raise
    click [X]                close (slide off before releasing to cancel)
    drag the title bar       move (Alt+drag anywhere also moves)
    drag an edge or corner   resize
    Alt+right-drag           resize from the nearest corner
    double-click title       maximize / restore
    middle-click title       lower the window


Starting programs
-----------------

There's no menu. Put what you want at login in ~/.mcgregor-wm/autostart
and make it executable (chmod +x):

    #!/bin/sh
    xsetroot -solid '#2d3a46' &
    xterm &

Everything else you start from an xterm (Alt+Return).


Restarting
----------

Press Ctrl+Alt+Shift+R, or from an xterm:

    killall -HUP mcgregor-wm

The window manager gives its windows back to X and starts again in the
same process, so X doesn't exit. It runs the binary that is installed at
that moment, so "make install" followed by a restart puts a new build into
effect. Windows keep their desktop, place, size, iconified and fullscreen
state, and the focus and Alt+Tab order are kept. The autostart file isn't
run again.


Configuration
-------------

Edit the block at the top of mcgregor-wm.c and run make again: number of
desktops, the modifier key (Mod1Mask is Alt), border width, title font
and padding, colours, the terminal command, and the new-window rules
(FOCUS_NEW_IF_NONE, FOCUS_TRANSIENTS, RAISE_NEW, WARP_ON_ALTTAB).

Every function in mcgregor-wm.c has a comment above it explaining what it
does.


How this build was checked
--------------------------

It was not compiled with gcc 2.7.2.3 or against XFree86 3.3 itself;
neither is available here. Instead:

  - it compiles cleanly as strict C89 (gcc -std=c89 -pedantic
    -Werror=declaration-after-statement), the dialect gcc 2.7.2.3
    implements;
  - every Xlib function it calls existed by X11R5, so all are in X11R6.3,
    and every C library function it uses is in libc5;
  - its behaviour was tested on a virtual X server with the same
    automated suites as the modern version: focus not stolen by new
    windows or by apps, Alt+Tab order, desktops, move, edge and corner
    resizing, maximize, fullscreen, iconify, close, restart (82 checks),
    plus a check that UTF-8 titles are converted to Latin-1 correctly.

If gcc 2.7.2.3 reports anything, send me the error and I'll fix it.
